<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domitian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">51–96 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Domitian was Roman emperor from 81 to 96 CE, the last of the Flavian dynasty. He strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier, fought wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a>, the Chatti and the Sarmatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>, and carried out a large building programme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His autocratic style and the treason trials of his later years made him hated by the senatorial class, and he was assassinated in a palace plot.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domitian.jpg" alt="Domitian" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domiziano_da_collezione_albani,_fine_del_I_sec._dc._02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Domitian was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 24 October 51 as the youngest son of Vespasian and Flavia Domitilla Major. He had an older sister, Domitilla the Younger, and an older brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a>. The Flavians had risen from relative obscurity to prominence in four generations, gaining wealth and status under the Julio-Claudian emperors. Domitian spent much of his youth in the shadow of Titus, who won military renown in the First Jewish-Roman War, while their father and brother were away commanding armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>.</p><p>After Vespasian became emperor in 69, Domitian received honours but no responsibilities. In 70 he persuaded Domitia Longina, daughter of the general Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo, to leave her husband and marry him. For about ten years his role stayed largely ceremonial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a>, Domitian was declared emperor by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a>. His reign of fifteen years was the longest since that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. He dropped the republican facade kept by his father and brother, moved the centre of government to the imperial court and drastically curtailed the powers of the Senate. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a>, he believed the empire should be governed as a divine monarchy. He took the office of perpetual censor to control public and private morals.</p><p>He revalued the Roman coinage, raising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> purity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denarius">denarius</a> from 90% to 98%. A financial crisis in 85 forced it down to 93.5%, still above the levels of Vespasian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a>. Around fifty structures were built, restored or completed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, among them the Stadium of Domitian, the Odeon of Domitian and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-jupiter-optimus-maximus">Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>, and the Flavian Palace on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatine-hill">Palatine Hill</a>.</p><p>His campaigns were mostly defensive. He developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a>, a network of roads, forts and watchtowers along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and founded the Legio I Minervia in 82. On 1 January 89 Lucius Antonius Saturninus, governor of Germania Superior, rose in revolt with two legions and the Chatti; the rising stayed within his province and was put down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Domitian was assassinated on 18 September 96 in a plot by court officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> gives the main role to the chamberlain Parthenius; the blow was struck by Parthenius' freedman Maximus and Stephanus, a steward of Domitian's niece Flavia Domitilla. The Senate proclaimed Nerva emperor the same day, and condemned Domitian's memory to oblivion.</p><p>Most surviving accounts come from senatorial or aristocratic writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>, who wrote after his death. Only the poets Martial and Statius, who praised him extravagantly, wrote during his reign. Since the 20th century, historians such as Brian W. Jones have described him as a ruthless but efficient autocrat whose policies laid foundations for the peaceful second century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domitian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Roman legion left a Latin inscription at Gobustan near Baku under the emperor <b>Domitian</b>, the easternmost Latin inscription known.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 89 <b>Domitian</b>, after killing their peace envoys, was defeated by the Marcomanni.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…around 107 BCE; the Iazyges, who settled between the Danube and the Tisza, destroyed a Roman legion in <b>Domitian</b>'s Sarmatian war of 92 CE; the Roxolani joined Decebalus of Dacia against Trajan; and in the Marcomannic Wars the Iazyges fought Marcus Aurelius until their king Zanticus made peace…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Vespasian's younger son <b>Domitian</b> remodelled it further, building the hypogeum and adding a gallery at the top to increase its capacity.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The Augustan Pantheon burned in 80 CE; <b>Domitian</b> rebuilt it, and it burned again in 110.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor <b>Domitian</b> was stabbed to death in a palace conspiracy, and the Senate chose the elderly Nerva to succeed him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-18">18 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=80" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Domitian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=80">What was here in 80 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the lot of princes was most unhappy, since when they discovered a conspiracy, no one believed them unless they had been killed.&#8221;</p><footer>Suetonius, Life of Domitian, 21 &#183; tr. J. C. Rolfe &#183; a saying of his, as Suetonius reports it &#183; <a href="https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Domitian*.html" rel="noopener">penelope.uchicago.edu</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
